{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "openwrt",
  "description": "A full ubus collection against a stock OpenWrt install. Every response shape here was CAPTURED FROM A REAL OpenWrt 24.10.8 (aarch64_generic rootfs container, image digest sha256:f6dd33c1d9b7...) over POST /ubus, then scrubbed: the board model, hostname, and MACs were replaced with router-shaped synthetic values, and the container's own eth0/docker0 addresses were replaced with a plausible LAN. The envelope, the acls block, the result:[0,payload] framing, the upper-case MACs, and the getDHCPLeases/getHostHints/getNetworkDevices field names are verbatim from that capture. Proves: session.login returns the session id at result[1].ubus_rpc_session; the ACL list in the login reply gates which objects are attempted; the router asset is built from system.board plus system.info plus getNetworkDevices; loopback (flags.loopback) and docker0 are dropped from the router's interfaces while br-lan is kept, because on OpenWrt the LAN bridge is where the device's real address lives; the router's OWN MAC appearing in getHostHints does not produce a second asset; one host seen by host hints, a DHCP lease, and a wireless association folds into ONE asset carrying all three sources; and a host whose only addresses are APIPA and IPv6 link-local imports with its MAC alone rather than with an address that identifies nothing. It also pins the one place the id and the interface deliberately DISAGREE: the locally administered MAC de:ad:be:ef:00:01 keeps its LAA bit in the foreign id, because the id must stay lossless, while the emitted NetworkInterface carries dc:ad:be:ef:00:01 because net.network_interface clears that bit to help cross-source matching. If a future change routed the id through normalize_mac, this assertion is what would catch it. no_mac_in_id is skipped: an observed host has no identifier but its MAC, and the id is deliberately paired with no-id-match no-id-break so it never drives a merge.",
